"Linearization Explains Fine-Tuning in Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08239v1)\nciteturn31646view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view2\",\"lineno\":130}); Total lines: 810\nL126: ###### Proof.\nL127: \nL128: See Appendix cite19†C . ∎\nL129: \nL130: Intuitively, one can think of (cite58†9 ) as placing the “average” $\\lambda$, $\\frac{1}{t}\\Lambda_{t}$ in (cite59†10 ). In what follows, we adhere to (cite59†10 ), for simplicity. Note that in conventional fine-tuning, $\\lambda=0$. Since $\\lim_{\\lambda\\to 0}\\frac{1-e^{-\\lambda t}}{\\lambda}=t$ the bound in (cite59†10 ) recovers\nL131: \nL132:  | $\\displaystyle\\|\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\|\\leq 2\\,\\text{Lip}(f)\\,\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\mathbf{Y}\\|\\,t,$  |  | (11)\nL133: which follows Theorem 2.3 in [cite48†16 ], in the case when the rescaling parameter is one. In other words, regularization and rescaling both manifest the lazy training phenomenon, but regularization maintains much closer proximity, since $\\frac{1-e^{-\\lambda t}}{\\lambda}$ is bounded, unlike $t$.\nL134: Theorem cite53†2 is primarily a building block for Theorem cite54†3 ; it simply says that while the solution may deviate from the origin under regularization, we can bound that deviation. Theorems cite54†3 and cite60†4 show that we can jump from a deviation from the initialization bound, which is the direct objective of regularization, to a bound on how far the NTK solution and the regularized fine-tuning solutions are, i.e., the indirect but main benefit of regularization.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Statistical Framework for Alignment with Biased AI Feedback (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08259v1)\nciteturn31646view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view3\",\"lineno\":98}); Total lines: 458\nL97: In RLHF with Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO, cite27†Schulman et al., 2017 ), one first fits a reward model $r_{\\phi}$ from preference data and then solves the KL-regularized control problem\nL98:  | $$\\max_{\\pi\\in\\Pi}\\Big\\{\\mathbb{E}_{X,Y\\sim\\pi(\\cdot\\mid X)}[r_{\\phi}(X,Y)]-\\beta\\,D_{\\mathrm{KL}}\\!\\big(\\pi(Y\\mid X)\\,\\|\\,\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y\\mid X)\\big)\\Big\\},$$  |  | (2.2)\nL99: \nL100: where $\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}$ is a fixed reference policy. $\\beta>0$ controls the regularization strength, and $\\Pi$ denotes the candidate LLM policy class.\nL101: Direct Preference Optimization (DPO; cite28†Rafailov et al., 2023 ) bypasses fitting a reward model by exploiting a closed-form characterization of the optimizer of (cite37†2.2 ) at the population level. Under standard regularity conditions, the optimal policy satisfies $\\pi(Y\\mid X)\\ \\propto\\ \\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y\\mid X)\\exp\\{\\beta^{-1}r_{\\phi}(X,Y)\\}$, which implies the reward–policy relation\nL102: \nL103:  | $$r_{\\phi}(X,Y)=\\beta\\log\\frac{\\pi(Y\\mid X)}{\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y\\mid X)}+\\beta\\log W(X),$$  |  | (2.3)\nL104: where $W(X)$ is a normalizing term and independent of $Y$. Substituting (cite38†2.3 ) into the Bradley–Terry likelihood yields the DPO objective\nL105: \nL106:  | $$L_{\\mathrm{DPO}}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}):=-\\mathbb{E}\\Bigg[\\log\\sigma\\Big(\\beta(2Z-1)\\big\\{\\Delta_{\\pi}(X;Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)})-\\Delta_{\\mathrm{ref}}(X;Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)})\\big\\}\\Big)\\Bigg],$$  |  | (2.4)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpherical Steering: Geometry-Aware Activation Rotation for Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08169v1)\nciteturn31646view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view0\",\"pattern\":\"C.1\"}); Total lines: 533\nL38:       1. cite28†4.4.1 Direction vs. Magnitude: Empirical Validation of Hyperspherical Geometry L47:   9. cite37†A Hyperparameters Interpretation L48:     1. cite38†A.1 Roles and Valid Ranges of $\\alpha,\\beta,\\kappa$ L49:       1. cite39†A.1.1 Selectivity threshold $\\beta\\in[-1,1)$ L50:       2. cite40†A.1.2 Rotation scale $\\alpha\\in(0,1]$ L51:       3. cite41†A.1.3 Why $\\kappa$ is often less sensitive L52:     2. cite42†A.2 Practical Tuning Recipe L53:   10. cite43†B Addtional Experiments on LLaMA-3.1-8B L54:   11. cite44†C Implementation Details L55:     1. cite45†C.1 Datasets and Splits L56:     2. cite46†C.2 Evaluation Metrics in Detail L57:     3. cite47†C.3 Supplementary Settings for Rank Analysis L58:   12. cite48†D Prompts L59:     1. cite49†D.1 BoolQ L60:     2. cite50†D.2 COPA L61:     3. cite51†D.3 StoryCloze L62:     4. cite52†D.4 MMLU L63:     5. cite53†D.5 WinoGrande L64:     6. cite54†D.6 TruthfulQA L65:     7. cite55†D.7 TruthfulQA (5-shot ICL) L66:   13. cite56†E Qualitative Examples L67: cite57†License: CC BY-NC-SA 4.0†info.arxiv.org L68: \nL69: arXiv:2602.08169v1 [cs.LG] 09 Feb 2026\nL70: # Spherical Steering: Geometry-Aware Activation Rotation for Language Models\nL71: \nL72: Zejia You Affiliation: Rice University Affiliation: Tufts University Email: Zejia.You@tufts.edu    Chunyuan Deng Affiliation: Rice University Email: chunyuan.deng@rice.edu    Hanjie Chen Affiliation: Rice University Email: hanjie@rice.edu\nL73: \nL74: ###### Abstract\nL457: This shows that our rotation-based update composes well with in-context demonstrations: rather than trading off open-ended quality for likelihood-based accuracy, it strengthens both capabilities under the same prompting setup.\nL458: Overall, these additional results on LLaMA-3.1-8B further support our central claim that activation rotation is a stronger inference-time control primitive than activation addition, and that it remains effective when combined with few-shot prompting.\nL459: ## Appendix C Implementation Details\nL460: \nL461: We provide our implementation details here.\nL462: ### C.1 Datasets and Splits\nL463: \nL464: We evaluate our models on six datasets covering different domains of open-ended QA and multiple-choice tasks.\nL465: \nL466:   * •\nL467: Choice of Plausible Alternatives (COPA) (cite118†Gordon et al., 2012 ) is a causal reasoning benchmark. Each example provides a short premise and two candidate alternatives; the model must choose the alternative that is more plausibly related to the premise via a causal relation (typically cause→effect or effect→cause). Because the two options are minimally different and both are plausible on the surface, COPA probes whether a model can rely on commonsense causal structure rather than shallow lexical cues.\nL468: We randomly divide develop split (400 examples), and split the training/ validation set by $4:1$. Using the rest of 100 examples for the test set.\nL469: \nL470:   * •\nL471: StoryCloze (cite119†Mostafazadeh et al., 2016 ) evaluates narrative understanding and script learning. Each instance contains a four-sentence story context and two candidate endings; the task is to select the ending that best completes the story in a coherent and commonsense way. Success requires tracking entities, events, and implicit temporal/causal consistency, making it a useful probe of higher-level discourse reasoning.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpherical Steering: Geometry-Aware Activation Rotation for Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08169v1)\nciteturn31646view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view0\",\"pattern\":\"hardware\"}); Total lines: 533\nNo matching text found for \"hardware\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpherical Steering: Geometry-Aware Activation Rotation for Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08169v1)\nciteturn31646view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view0\",\"pattern\":\"C.2\"}); Total lines: 533\nL47:   9. cite37†A Hyperparameters Interpretation L48:     1. cite38†A.1 Roles and Valid Ranges of $\\alpha,\\beta,\\kappa$ L49:       1. cite39†A.1.1 Selectivity threshold $\\beta\\in[-1,1)$ L50:       2. cite40†A.1.2 Rotation scale $\\alpha\\in(0,1]$ L51:       3. cite41†A.1.3 Why $\\kappa$ is often less sensitive L52:     2. cite42†A.2 Practical Tuning Recipe L53:   10. cite43†B Addtional Experiments on LLaMA-3.1-8B L54:   11. cite44†C Implementation Details L55:     1. cite45†C.1 Datasets and Splits L56:     2. cite46†C.2 Evaluation Metrics in Detail L57:     3. cite47†C.3 Supplementary Settings for Rank Analysis L58:   12. cite48†D Prompts L59:     1. cite49†D.1 BoolQ L60:     2. cite50†D.2 COPA L61:     3. cite51†D.3 StoryCloze L62:     4. cite52†D.4 MMLU L63:     5. cite53†D.5 WinoGrande L64:     6. cite54†D.6 TruthfulQA L65:     7. cite55†D.7 TruthfulQA (5-shot ICL) L66:   13. cite56†E Qualitative Examples L67: cite57†License: CC BY-NC-SA 4.0†info.arxiv.org L68: \nL69: arXiv:2602.08169v1 [cs.LG] 09 Feb 2026\nL70: # Spherical Steering: Geometry-Aware Activation Rotation for Language Models\nL71: \nL72: Zejia You Affiliation: Rice University Affiliation: Tufts University Email: Zejia.You@tufts.edu    Chunyuan Deng Affiliation: Rice University Email: chunyuan.deng@rice.edu    Hanjie Chen Affiliation: Rice University Email: hanjie@rice.edu\nL73: \nL74: ###### Abstract\nL229: We evaluate on two complementary task categories: (1) Multiple-choice reasoning, where models select the most plausible answer from a fixed set of candidates using likelihood scoring. We assess performance on TruthfulQA (MC1/MC2/MC3) (cite117†Lin et al., 2022 ), COPA (cite118†Gordon et al., 2012 ), StoryCloze (cite119†Mostafazadeh et al., 2016 ), BoolQ (cite120†Clark et al., 2019 ), MMLU (cite121†Hendrycks et al., 2020 ), and WinoGrande (cite122†Sakaguchi et al., 2020 ).\nL230: (2) Open-ended generation, where models produce free-form answers. We evaluate using TruthfulQA’s generative setting with two pretrained judge models measuring truthfulness (TRUE) and informativeness (INFO), with TRUE$\\times$INFO serving as the primary metric (cite79†Li et al., 2023 ). Please refer to Appendix cite46†C.2 for detailed experimental setups.\nL231: ##### Models.\nL232: \nL233: We evaluate on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct (cite91†Yang et al., 2024 ) and LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (cite60†Dubey et al., 2024 ), two state-of-the-art open-source instruction-tuned models with distinct architectural characteristics.\nL234: ##### Baselines.\nL235: We compare against representative activation steering methods: (1) Inference-Time Intervention (ITI) (cite79†Li et al., 2023 ), which uses linear probes to identify steering heads; (2) Contrastive Activation Addition (CAA) (cite80†Rimsky et al., 2024 ), which applies layer-wise additive interventions; (3) SADI-HEAD (cite102†Wang et al., 2024 ), which uses dynamic element-wise scaling at attention heads; and (4) 5-shot In-Context Learning (ICL) as a prompt-only baseline.\nL487: TruthfulQA (cite117†Lin et al., 2022 ) evaluates whether a language model produces truthful and non-misleading responses, particularly for questions that trigger common misconceptions. It contains 817 questions across 38 categories (e.g., health, law, finance, politics). TruthfulQA provides both a multiple-choice track and an open-ended generation track.\nL488: We use 2 folds cross-validation over the 817 questions. For each fold, randomly split the training / validation set by $4:1$, building prototypes using the training set only.\nL489: ### C.2 Evaluation Metrics in Detail\nL490: In Table cite93†1 we follow the official TruthfulQA (cite117†Lin et al., 2022 ) multiple-choice evaluation by scoring each reference answer with its conditional log-likelihood (sum of token log-probabilities). MC1 reports whether the best correct answer outranks all incorrect answers; MC3 reports the fraction of correct answers that outrank the top incorrect answer; and MC2 reports the normalized probability mass assigned to correct answers after exponentiating and normalizing scores over all candidates.\nL491: For open-ended generation, we employ two pre-trained judge models to judge the truthfulness^{1}^{1} 1 https://huggingface.co/allenai/truthfulqa-truth-judge-llama2-7B and informativeness^{2}^{2} 2 https://huggingface.co/allenai/truthfulqa-info-judge-llama2-7B denoted as TRUE (%) and INFO (%), respectively. Following the prior work (cite79†Li et al., 2023 ), we use their product TRUE$\\times$INFO (%) as the primary metric.\nL492: For other multiple-choice evaluation benchmarks, we consider COPA (cite118†Gordon et al., 2012 ), StoryCloze (cite119†Mostafazadeh et al., 2016 ), BoolQ (cite120†Clark et al., 2019 ), MMLU (cite121†Hendrycks et al., 2020 ), and WinoGrande (cite122†Sakaguchi et al., 2020 ), covering answer options ranging from 2-way to 4-way.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPretraining with Token-Level Adaptive Latent Chain-of-Thought (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08220v1)\nciteturn31646view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view1\",\"pattern\":\"FLOPs\"}); Total lines: 343\nL66: We propose Pretraining with Token-Level Adaptive Latent CoT (adaptive latent CoT), where the model generates a variable-length latent CoT trajectory before emitting each token—allocating longer trajectories to difficult tokens and shorter (or even zero) trajectories to easy ones. Importantly, this behavior emerges naturally from one-stage pretraining on general text and reduces computation in both training and inference via token-wise adaptive halting.\nL67: Experiments with Llama architectures show that adaptive latent CoT consistently improves language modeling perplexity and broad downstream accuracy, even with fewer training FLOPs than prior recurrent baselines.\nL68: ## 1 Introduction\nL69: The remarkable success of Large Language Models (LLMs) has been primarily driven by scaling up model parameters and training data (cite46†Kaplan et al., 2020 ; cite47†Liu et al., 2024 ; cite48†Brown et al., 2020 ).\nL70: However, this paradigm is approaching critical bottlenecks: the exhaustion of high-quality public data (cite49†Muennighoff et al., 2023 ; cite50†Villalobos et al., 2022 ) and the substantial communication overheads associated with scaling model size (cite51†Pati et al., 2023 ; cite52†Narayanan et al., 2021 ; cite53†Hoffmann et al., 2022 ). Consequently, a key research direction is to improve model capability under a fixed parameter and data budget by scaling compute per token.\nL71: To scale compute without expanding parameters, prior work has explored parameter-sharing strategies that emulate greater depth by recursively reusing the same layer weights (cite54†Zeng et al., 2025b ; cite55†Giannou et al., 2023 ; cite56†Zhu et al., 2025 ). While appealing, this form of depth scaling can be prone to training instability.\nL72: More recently, PonderLM2 (cite57†Zeng et al., 2025a ) introduces latent computation before predicting the next token, but parallelizing this process often requires Jacobi-style iteration and incurs substantial training FLOPs. Moreover, these approaches typically allocate uniform compute to all tokens, which is inherently suboptimal compared to human cognition where thinking is adaptive—brief for simple concepts and prolonged for complex reasoning.\nL73: Parallel to architectural modifications, another line of research has demonstrated that scaling inference-time compute—via CoT (cite58†Wei et al., 2022 )—has achieved remarkable success. However, this capability typically relies on explicit supervision (annotated CoT data), is confined to a discrete token space, and is ultimately capped by the base model’s pre-trained capabilities (cite59†Yue et al., 2025 ).\nL90: Moreover, methods like Ouro, PonderNet, and CALM (cite56†Zhu et al., 2025 ; cite63†Banino et al., 2021 ; cite61†Schuster et al., 2022 ) typically still require full computation during training, so they do not reduce training FLOPs and mainly save compute at inference.\nL109: While this enables parallelism, the iterative refinement substantially increases training compute—for example, even with a latent step budget of $K=1$, it can incur around $8\\times$ the training FLOPs of the vanilla model.\nL110: ### 3.2 Parallel Latent Steps via Parallel Masking\nL111: \nL112: To enable parallel computation while maintaining training efficiency, we expand attention into two dimensions: sequence position ($t$) and latent step ($k$). We define a Parallel Attention Mask over 2D indices $(t,k)$ that enforces strict causality in both dimensions:\nL113: \nL114:  | $$M_{(t_{i},k_{i}),(t_{j},k_{j})}=\\begin{cases}0&\\text{if }t_{j}\\leq t_{i}\\ \\land\\ k_{j}\\leq k_{i},\\\\\nL115: -\\infty&\\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}$$  |  | (2)\nL142: ##### Truncation (threshold pruning).\nL143: \nL144: To save FLOPs, we avoid executing latent steps that are unlikely to be reached. After computing $g_{t}^{(k)}$, we estimate the probability of reaching the next step via $p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(k+1)}$. If $p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(k+1)}<\\tau$, we prune token $t$ from the batch for all subsequent steps. Equivalently, we define the last executed step:\nL145: \nL146:  | $$K_{t}^{\\star}\\triangleq\\max\\Bigl\\{k\\leq K_{\\max}\\ \\bigm|\\ p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(k)}\\geq\\tau\\Bigr\\}.$$  |  | (6)\nL157: ### 3.4 Correctness-Aware Adaptive Loss\nL158: \nL159: While probabilistic halting enables token-wise adaptive computation, we additionally need to guide the Router to stop when extra computation is unlikely to help. As evidenced in cite79†Figure 2 , once $p_{\\mathrm{target}}^{(k)}$ is high, additional latent steps bring little benefit and can even be detrimental. We therefore propose a Correctness-Aware Adaptive Loss that encourages early halting for tokens that are already likely correct, reducing overall FLOPs. Let\nL177: At step 1, the model produces $(z_{1}^{(1)},z_{2}^{(1)},z_{3}^{(1)})$ and the Router outputs gates (conditional continuation probabilities) for each token, which update $p_{\\mathrm{reach}}$ and determine whether a token remains active. In the figure, $x_{1},x_{2}$ keep sufficient reach probability to proceed (green checks), while $x_{3}$ is pruned (red cross). At step 2, only $x_{1},x_{2}$ remain; the updated reach probability prunes $x_{2}$, so steps 3–4 are executed only for $x_{1}$.\nL178: Across latent steps, we reuse the KV cache so later steps reuse cached attention context, avoiding redundant computation. Since pruning shrinks the active token set as $k$ increases, the overall training FLOPs decrease.\nL179: For supervision, we compute per-position LM losses ($\\mathcal{L}_{1},\\mathcal{L}_{2},\\mathcal{L}_{3}$) on the final representation $z_{t}^{\\mathrm{f}}$ obtained by $p_{\\mathrm{exit}}$-weighted mixing of the executed latent states, and add a correctness-aware adaptive loss (proportional to $p_{\\mathrm{target}}$) to penalize continuing when the model already assigns high probability to the ground-truth token, the $p_{\\mathrm{exit}}$-weighted mixing and $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{adaptive}}$ provide learning signals to both the LM and the Router.\nL180: We unroll latent computation for $k=1,\\ldots,K_{\\max}$ under the parallel attention mask. At each latent step, all active token positions are processed in parallel over $t$. To improve efficiency, we reuse the KV cache across latent steps to avoid recomputing shared attention context, and apply threshold pruning so the active token set shrinks as $k$ increases, reducing overall training FLOPs (cite83†Figure 4 ).\nL181: The Router is trained jointly through (i) gradients from the main cross-entropy loss computed on the mixed representation $z^{\\mathrm{final}}$ and (ii) the correctness-aware adaptive penalty that discourages unnecessary continuation.\nL182: ##### Inference.\nL183: \nL184: We use the same Router to perform token-wise halting during decoding. For each position, we iteratively compute latent states and update the reach probability, stopping when the next-step reach probability falls below a threshold $\\tau$. We then form $z^{\\mathrm{final}}$ via the (truncated) $p_{\\mathrm{exit}}$-weighted mixing of the executed latent states and emit the next token with the LM head (cite84†Figure 3 ).\nL185: ### 3.6 Positional Embedding\nL186: \nL187: All latent-step states $z_{t}^{(k)}$ share the same positional embedding (position id) as the input token $x_{t}$ at position $t$, rather than using new positions. Thus, latent steps do not consume extra position ids, and the effective context window stays unchanged from the vanilla model.\nL188: \nL189: ## 4 Experiments\nL190: \nL191: ### 4.1 Setup\nL192: ##### Training Setup and Baselines.\nL193: We train all models from scratch using the LLaMA architecture (cite85†Dubey et al., 2024 ). Specifically, we pretrain two model sizes (410M and 1.4B parameters) for 26B tokens on the Pile over 50,000 optimization steps, following the protocol of cite57†Zeng et al. (2025a) .\nL194: To ensure a controlled comparison, we train a suite of baselines under an identical pretraining protocol—matching the training data, optimization schedule, and hyperparameters—including vanilla LLaMA models as well as competitive compute-scaling and recurrent variants: LoopedLM (cite55†Giannou et al., 2023 ), PausedLM (cite67†Goyal et al., 2023 ), PonderLM (cite54†Zeng et al., 2025b ), PonderLM2 (cite57†Zeng et al., 2025a ), and the adaptive Mixture-of-Recurrence (MoR) method (cite66†Bae et al., 2025 ).\nL195: cite86†Image: Refer to caption Figure 5: Language modeling perplexity (PPL $\\downarrow$). Validation perplexity on The Pile, WikiText, and LAMBADA (OpenAI and standard splits). Our method consistently achieves the lowest perplexity across all datasets while using fewer training FLOPs.\nL196: ##### Evaluation.\nL197: We evaluate language modeling quality using validation perplexity on The Pile (cite87†Gao et al., 2020 ), WikiText (cite88†Merity et al., 2016 ), and LAMBADA (cite89†Paperno et al., 2016 ) (both the OpenAI and standard splits).\nL198: To assess downstream generalization, we follow the evaluation protocol of cite90†Gu and Dao (2024) and report 0-shot and 5-shot accuracy on a broad benchmark suite, including LAMBADA (cite89†Paperno et al., 2016 ), SciQ (cite91†Welbl et al., 2017 ), HellaSwag (cite92†Zellers et al., 2019 ), PIQA (cite93†Bisk et al., 2020 ), WinoGrande (cite94†Sakaguchi et al., 2021 ), ARC-Easy and ARC-Challenge (cite95†Clark et al., 2018 ), and RACE (cite96†Lai et al., 2017 ).\nL199: ### 4.2 Main Results\nL200: \nL201: ##### Language Modeling Ability.\nL202: \nL203: As shown in cite97†Figure 5 , our method consistently achieves the lowest perplexity across all datasets while using the least training FLOPs. Specifically, our LLaMA-1.4B model with max latent CoT length $\\ell_{\\max}=3$ outperforms the strongest baseline (PonderLM-2) while requiring less than half of the training compute (7.47 vs. 17.47 $\\times 10^{20}$ FLOPs). Moreover, increasing $\\ell_{\\max}$ to 5 further improves performance.\nL204: ##### Downstream Tasks.\nL205: \nL206: Across both zero-shot and five-shot settings and under all compute budgets, our method achieves the best overall average accuracy (cite98†Table 1 ). Notably, our LLaMA-410M with $\\ell_{\\max}=3$ outperforms the compute-comparable vanilla LLaMA-1.4B baseline in average accuracy for both zero-shot and five-shot, indicating that our approach can be even more effective than simply scaling up the parameter count under a matched compute budget.\nL207: Table 1: Zero-shot and five-shot accuracy (%) on downstream tasks. FLOPs denote total pretraining compute (in units of $\\times 10^{20}$), estimated following cite46†Kaplan et al. (2020) . Rows are grouped by compute budget. ^{†} denotes a vanilla baseline with comparable compute.\nL208: Model  | FLOPs  | Lambada (OpenAI)  | Lambada (Std)  | ARC -C  | ARC -E  | Wino Grande  | PIQA  | Hella Swag  | SciQ  | RACE  | Avg. Acc.\nL209: Zero-shot Performance\nL210: Looped LLaMA-410M (4 loops)  | 2.56  | 47.1  | 34.9  | 22.6  | 50.9  | 51.2  | 65.5  | 32.6  | 79.5  | 30.4  | 46.1\nL211: Pause LLaMA-410M (3 pauses)  | 2.56  | 46.3  | 35.3  | 20.9  | 49.9  | 51.7  | 64.7  | 31.7  | 80.1  | 30.4  | 45.7\nL212: PonderLM-410M (3 steps)  | 2.56  | 48.9  | 36.9  | 21.2  | 50.4  | 54.1  | 66.3  | 33.3  | 80.6  | 30.4  | 46.9\nL213: PonderLM2-410M (1 step)  | 5.12  | 48.7  | 37.7  | 21.0  | 50.6  | 51.3  | 65.1  | 33.0  | 79.8  | 30.8  | 46.4\nL214: MoR-410M (4 Recursions)  | 2.27  | 43.1  | 32.0  | 19.8  | 49.5  | 51.7  | 64.9  | 31.1  | 80.8  | 30.9  | 44.9\nL215: LLaMA-1.4B (train from scratch)^{†}  | 2.18  | 50.6  | 37.1  | 20.9  | 52.2  | 53.0  | 65.6  | 33.6  | 84.5  | 31.8  | 47.7\nL216: Our LLaMA-410M ($\\ell_{\\max}=3$)  | 2.27  | 49.3  | 40.2  | 23.6  | 50.8  | 53.7  | 65.9  | 33.9  | 82.2  | 33.0  | 48.1\nL217: Looped LLaMA-1.4B (4 loops)  | 8.74  | 55.8  | 45.6  | 23.2  | 55.2  | 54.1  | 68.9  | 37.6  | 84.9  | 33.0  | 50.9\nL218: Pause LLaMA-1.4B (3 pauses)  | 8.74  | 56.2  | 46.5  | 24.2  | 54.0  | 55.3  | 68.8  | 36.7  | 85.4  | 32.3  | 51.0\nL219: PonderLM-1.4B (3 steps)  | 8.74  | 56.7  | 45.4  | 23.8  | 56.3  | 55.6  | 68.3  | 37.8  | 86.3  | 33.0  | 51.5\nL220: Our LLaMA-1.4B ($\\ell_{\\max}=3$)  | 7.47  | 58.8  | 49.4  | 25.8  | 57.1  | 57.4  | 69.2  | 39.3  | 87.2  | 33.4  | 53.1\nL221: PonderLM-1.4B (4 steps)  | 10.92  | 56.2  | 45.8  | 24.9  | 54.5  | 54.7  | 69.0  | 38.1  | 84.3  | 35.4  | 51.4\nL222: PonderLM2-1.4B (1 step)  | 17.47  | 58.1  | 48.2  | 25.2  | 58.0  | 53.9  | 70.7  | 38.6  | 85.9  | 32.4  | 52.3\nL223: Our LLaMA-1.4B ($\\ell_{\\max}=5$)  | 10.84  | 61.0  | 52.7  | 25.9  | 59.2  | 57.6  | 70.4  | 40.9  | 87.1  | 35.3  | 54.5\nL224: Five-shot Performance\nL225: Looped LLaMA-410M (4 loops)  | 2.56  | 42.0  | 33.6  | 22.7  | 52.2  | 50.9  | 66.2  | 33.0  | 86.9  | 30.5  | 46.4\nL244: In cite101†Figure 6 , we compare four 410M-parameter models (LoopedLM, PausedLM, PonderLM, and ours) and report their validation loss under two alignment settings: (i) iso-data, where checkpoints are matched by the same token/step budget, and (ii) iso-FLOPs, where checkpoints are matched by the same estimated total training compute. Across both settings and all budgets, our method consistently achieves the lowest validation loss.\n"
